{"title":"Block walking","text":"<div class=\"mw-content-ltr mw-parser-output\" lang=\"en\" dir=\"ltr\"><div id=\"toc\" class=\"toc\" role=\"navigation\" aria-labelledby=\"mw-toc-heading\"><input type=\"checkbox\" role=\"button\" id=\"toctogglecheckbox\" class=\"toctogglecheckbox\" style=\"display:none\" /><div class=\"toctitle\" lang=\"en\" dir=\"ltr\"><h2 id=\"mw-toc-heading\">Contents</h2><span class=\"toctogglespan\"><label class=\"toctogglelabel\" for=\"toctogglecheckbox\"></label></span></div>\n<ul>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-1\"><a href=\"#Example_problem\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">1</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Example problem</span></a>\n<ul>\n<li class=\"toclevel-2 tocsection-2\"><a href=\"#Solution\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">1.1</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Solution</span></a></li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-3\"><a href=\"#How_does_block_walking_even_work?\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">2</span> <span class=\"toctext\">How does block walking even work?</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-4\"><a href=\"#A_Question_to_think_about\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">3</span> <span class=\"toctext\">A Question to think about</span></a></li>\n</ul>\n</div>\n\n<h1><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Example_problem\">Example problem</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Block_walking&amp;action=edit&amp;section=1\" title=\"Edit section: Example problem\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h1>\n<p>If you can move only down or right, and you cannot go outside the boundaries of the lines, what is the number of ways you can get from <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/a/4/6a47ca0fe7cb276abc022af6ac88ddae1a9d6894.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$X$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"18\" height=\"14\" /> to <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/e/5/ce58e4af225c93d08606c26554caaa5ae32edeba.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$Y$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"16\" height=\"14\" />? \n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution\">Solution</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Block_walking&amp;action=edit&amp;section=2\" title=\"Edit section: Solution\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/1/6/d163c027d946bd5e811a190158643a728f96c177.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"[asy] label(&quot;X&quot;, (0,3), NW); label(&quot;Y&quot;, (3,0), SE); Draw((0,0) -- (3,0) -- (3,1) -- (3,3) -- (1,3) -- (0,3) -- cycle, linewidth(1)); Draw((1,0)--(1,3), linewidth(1)); Draw((2,0)--(2,3), linewidth(1)); Draw((3,0)--(3,3), linewidth(1)); Draw((0,1)--(3,1), linewidth(1)); Draw((0,2)--(3,2), linewidth(1)); Draw((0,3)--(3,3), linewidth(1)); [/asy]\" width=\"252\" height=\"248\" />\n</p><p>We are going to move either right or down.  We start off with some easier means of this problem from A to B by labeling closer distances.  Let's label these points with letters.\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/6/0/360bc68ea5cb93f2b634096430c0ce75ab58be91.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"[asy] label(&quot;X&quot;, (0,3), NW); label(&quot;Y&quot;, (3,0), SE); label(&quot;C&quot;, (2,0), SE); label(&quot;D&quot;, (1,0), SW); label(&quot;E&quot;, (0,0), SW); label(&quot;F&quot;, (0,1), SW); label(&quot;G&quot;, (0,2), NW); label(&quot;H&quot;, (1,1), SW); label(&quot;I&quot;, (2,1), SE); label(&quot;J&quot;, (3,1), SE); label(&quot;K&quot;, (3,2), NE); label(&quot;L&quot;, (3,3), NE); label(&quot;M&quot;, (2,2), NE); label(&quot;N&quot;, (2,3), NE); label(&quot;O&quot;, (1,3), NW); label(&quot;P&quot;, (1,2), NW); Draw((0,0) -- (3,0) -- (3,1) -- (3,3) -- (1,3) -- (0,3) -- cycle, linewidth(1)); Draw((1,0)--(1,3), linewidth(1)); Draw((2,0)--(2,3), linewidth(1)); Draw((3,0)--(3,3), linewidth(1)); Draw((0,1)--(3,1), linewidth(1)); Draw((0,2)--(3,2), linewidth(1)); Draw((0,3)--(3,3), linewidth(1)); [/asy]\" width=\"252\" height=\"248\" />\n</p><p>We first find that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/3/4/93426eb40711538867bc4a586083476922b95f7f.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\overline{YJ}$\" width=\"26\" height=\"15\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/a/b/eabe1c907abe2e2b90e0f556d3115edfadbf4075.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\overline{YC}$\" width=\"29\" height=\"15\" /> which is 1 for both of them \"for <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/3/4/93426eb40711538867bc4a586083476922b95f7f.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\overline{YJ}$\" width=\"26\" height=\"15\" /> we have to move down, and for <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/a/b/eabe1c907abe2e2b90e0f556d3115edfadbf4075.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\overline{YC}$\" width=\"29\" height=\"15\" /> we move right 1\".  \n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/9/8/8985ff312dc633a1389a2fdc367f291dc5d89a57.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"[asy] label(&quot;X&quot;, (0,3), NW); label(&quot;Y&quot;, (3,0), SE); label(&quot;1&quot;, (2,0), SE); label(&quot;D&quot;, (1,0), SW); label(&quot;E&quot;, (0,0), SW); label(&quot;F&quot;, (0,1), SW); label(&quot;G&quot;, (0,2), NW); label(&quot;H&quot;, (1,1), SW); label(&quot;I&quot;, (2,1), SE); label(&quot;1&quot;, (3,1), SE); label(&quot;K&quot;, (3,2), NE); label(&quot;L&quot;, (3,3), NE); label(&quot;M&quot;, (2,2), NE); label(&quot;N&quot;, (2,3), NE); label(&quot;O&quot;, (1,3), NW); label(&quot;P&quot;, (1,2), NW); Draw((0,0) -- (3,0) -- (3,1) -- (3,3) -- (1,3) -- (0,3) -- cycle, linewidth(1)); Draw((1,0)--(1,3), linewidth(1)); Draw((2,0)--(2,3), linewidth(1)); Draw((3,0)--(3,3), linewidth(1)); Draw((0,1)--(3,1), linewidth(1)); Draw((0,2)--(3,2), linewidth(1)); Draw((0,3)--(3,3), linewidth(1)); [/asy]\" width=\"252\" height=\"248\" />\n</p><p>For <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/9/a/d9ac3749a1ff187b11e1f40fb025770e4f84b132.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$YI$\" width=\"24\" height=\"12\" /> she is going to have to either go right or down to start off with when we go right, we already know how many ways we have, which would be 1.  For down, we again know how many ways we have, which is 1.  Therefore for <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/9/a/d9ac3749a1ff187b11e1f40fb025770e4f84b132.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$YI$\" width=\"24\" height=\"12\" /> we have 2 total ways.  \n</p><p><br />\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/1/7/517d0a78d9ee5f9e0185650096ccefd39b45b5f1.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"[asy] label(&quot;X&quot;, (0,3), NW); label(&quot;Y&quot;, (3,0), SE); label(&quot;1&quot;, (2,0), SE); label(&quot;D&quot;, (1,0), SW); label(&quot;E&quot;, (0,0), SW); label(&quot;F&quot;, (0,1), SW); label(&quot;G&quot;, (0,2), NW); label(&quot;H&quot;, (1,1), SW); label(&quot;2&quot;, (2,1), SE); label(&quot;1&quot;, (3,1), SE); label(&quot;K&quot;, (3,2), NE); label(&quot;L&quot;, (3,3), NE); label(&quot;M&quot;, (2,2), NE); label(&quot;N&quot;, (2,3), NE); label(&quot;O&quot;, (1,3), NW); label(&quot;P&quot;, (1,2), NW); Draw((0,0) -- (3,0) -- (3,1) -- (3,3) -- (1,3) -- (0,3) -- cycle, linewidth(1)); Draw((1,0)--(1,3), linewidth(1)); Draw((2,0)--(2,3), linewidth(1)); Draw((3,0)--(3,3), linewidth(1)); Draw((0,1)--(3,1), linewidth(1)); Draw((0,2)--(3,2), linewidth(1)); Draw((0,3)--(3,3), linewidth(1)); [/asy]\" width=\"252\" height=\"248\" />\n</p><p>We also know for <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/5/d/55d254f7545ef3a1b556d6e1509e71c62984bc1b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$KY, LY, DY, EY$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"132\" height=\"16\" /> we are going to have to go 2 or 3 down/right in both cases, which is a total of 1.  \n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/d/3/5d38765d8389679850d42eb6c0277377c5431d78.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"[asy] label(&quot;X&quot;, (0,3), NW); label(&quot;Y&quot;, (3,0), SE); label(&quot;1&quot;, (2,0), SE); label(&quot;1&quot;, (1,0), SW); label(&quot;1&quot;, (0,0), SW); label(&quot;F&quot;, (0,1), SW); label(&quot;G&quot;, (0,2), NW); label(&quot;H&quot;, (1,1), SW); label(&quot;2&quot;, (2,1), SE); label(&quot;1&quot;, (3,1), SE); label(&quot;1&quot;, (3,2), NE); label(&quot;1&quot;, (3,3), NE); label(&quot;M&quot;, (2,2), NE); label(&quot;N&quot;, (2,3), NE); label(&quot;O&quot;, (1,3), NW); label(&quot;P&quot;, (1,2), NW); Draw((0,0) -- (3,0) -- (3,1) -- (3,3) -- (1,3) -- (0,3) -- cycle, linewidth(1)); Draw((1,0)--(1,3), linewidth(1)); Draw((2,0)--(2,3), linewidth(1)); Draw((3,0)--(3,3), linewidth(1)); Draw((0,1)--(3,1), linewidth(1)); Draw((0,2)--(3,2), linewidth(1)); Draw((0,3)--(3,3), linewidth(1)); [/asy]\" width=\"252\" height=\"248\" />\n</p><p>Using this same process, we find for <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/9/9/f996e2c8cd749cec374c35c1edf3b111653ba484.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$MY$\" width=\"33\" height=\"12\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/0/3/2032e1e556f84d1b68572f85b2757e996c8fea4b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$HY$\" width=\"30\" height=\"12\" /> we are going to have to go down or right.  From there, we already have the numbers filled in.  For <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/9/9/f996e2c8cd749cec374c35c1edf3b111653ba484.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$MY$\" width=\"33\" height=\"12\" /> it is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/5/3/3537ecc982707e8637f03c8470642bbcd57a2ab5.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$1+2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"40\" height=\"13\" />, and for <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/0/3/2032e1e556f84d1b68572f85b2757e996c8fea4b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$HY$\" width=\"30\" height=\"12\" /> is it <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/0/2/a021347acd933e8bb33455fa2875bdc548bf3ad4.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2+1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"42\" height=\"15\" /> which in both cases is 3. \n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/4/b/94b05d3abf1e8ce75c3dfd1b094fd4a26c16cf82.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"[asy] label(&quot;X&quot;, (0,3), NW); label(&quot;Y&quot;, (3,0), SE); label(&quot;1&quot;, (2,0), SE); label(&quot;1&quot;, (1,0), SW); label(&quot;1&quot;, (0,0), SW); label(&quot;F&quot;, (0,1), SW); label(&quot;G&quot;, (0,2), NW); label(&quot;3&quot;, (1,1), SW); label(&quot;2&quot;, (2,1), SE); label(&quot;1&quot;, (3,1), SE); label(&quot;1&quot;, (3,2), NE); label(&quot;1&quot;, (3,3), NE); label(&quot;3&quot;, (2,2), NE); label(&quot;N&quot;, (2,3), NE); label(&quot;O&quot;, (1,3), NW); label(&quot;P&quot;, (1,2), NW); Draw((0,0) -- (3,0) -- (3,1) -- (3,3) -- (1,3) -- (0,3) -- cycle, linewidth(1)); Draw((1,0)--(1,3), linewidth(1)); Draw((2,0)--(2,3), linewidth(1)); Draw((3,0)--(3,3), linewidth(1)); Draw((0,1)--(3,1), linewidth(1)); Draw((0,2)--(3,2), linewidth(1)); Draw((0,3)--(3,3), linewidth(1)); [/asy]\" width=\"252\" height=\"248\" />\n</p><p>For <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/6/3/263b96ef7da892d764515a9107812fad1a92ab2f.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$NY$\" width=\"30\" height=\"12\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/9/c/a9c6cc1d5c36f73da5a94f00e4ea8bee08b68863.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$FY$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"31\" height=\"14\" /> we have to move down or right, and find we have <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/3/7/03704ef34efaa70bb2555dcd260b84f1d14e0cae.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$3+1=4$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"73\" height=\"14\" /> total ways once we move down/right.  \n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/3/6/d3616409fc1f76d6a6b832ab6ade8096009c587a.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"[asy] label(&quot;X&quot;, (0,3), NW); label(&quot;Y&quot;, (3,0), SE); label(&quot;1&quot;, (2,0), SE); label(&quot;1&quot;, (1,0), SW); label(&quot;1&quot;, (0,0), SW); label(&quot;4&quot;, (0,1), SW); label(&quot;G&quot;, (0,2), NW); label(&quot;3&quot;, (1,1), SW); label(&quot;2&quot;, (2,1), SE); label(&quot;1&quot;, (3,1), SE); label(&quot;1&quot;, (3,2), NE); label(&quot;1&quot;, (3,3), NE); label(&quot;3&quot;, (2,2), NE); label(&quot;4&quot;, (2,3), NE); label(&quot;O&quot;, (1,3), NW); label(&quot;P&quot;, (1,2), NW); Draw((0,0) -- (3,0) -- (3,1) -- (3,3) -- (1,3) -- (0,3) -- cycle, linewidth(1)); Draw((1,0)--(1,3), linewidth(1)); Draw((2,0)--(2,3), linewidth(1)); Draw((3,0)--(3,3), linewidth(1)); Draw((0,1)--(3,1), linewidth(1)); Draw((0,2)--(3,2), linewidth(1)); Draw((0,3)--(3,3), linewidth(1)); [/asy]\" width=\"252\" height=\"248\" />\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/e/9/4e99ebdaa2b11d68e402f790ddc591b59c7ae4a4.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$YP$\" width=\"28\" height=\"12\" /> is going to have the two cases of going down/right again, which is going to give us <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/d/f/6df768b2b37bfea85c62dfd671b8578ed1a76872.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$3+3=6$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"73\" height=\"14\" />\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/f/f/5ffa8299acd7698cfed89389366c79c603c3d331.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"[asy] label(&quot;X&quot;, (0,3), NW); label(&quot;Y&quot;, (3,0), SE); label(&quot;1&quot;, (2,0), SE); label(&quot;1&quot;, (1,0), SW); label(&quot;1&quot;, (0,0), SW); label(&quot;4&quot;, (0,1), SW); label(&quot;G&quot;, (0,2), NW); label(&quot;3&quot;, (1,1), SW); label(&quot;2&quot;, (2,1), SE); label(&quot;1&quot;, (3,1), SE); label(&quot;1&quot;, (3,2), NE); label(&quot;1&quot;, (3,3), NE); label(&quot;3&quot;, (2,2), NE); label(&quot;4&quot;, (2,3), NE); label(&quot;O&quot;, (1,3), NW); label(&quot;6&quot;, (1,2), NW); Draw((0,0) -- (3,0) -- (3,1) -- (3,3) -- (1,3) -- (0,3) -- cycle, linewidth(1)); Draw((1,0)--(1,3), linewidth(1)); Draw((2,0)--(2,3), linewidth(1)); Draw((3,0)--(3,3), linewidth(1)); Draw((0,1)--(3,1), linewidth(1)); Draw((0,2)--(3,2), linewidth(1)); Draw((0,3)--(3,3), linewidth(1)); [/asy]\" width=\"252\" height=\"248\" />\n</p><p>For <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/2/c/22c167afecebdcb56ed8b53ee1353ebff986f9d1.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$GY$\" width=\"28\" height=\"12\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/a/f/8afe773cd5709ee6b1f9f84680829802825cfab9.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$OY$\" width=\"28\" height=\"12\" /> we are going to have 6+4=10 total ways (since we start going down and right, and the cases have already been done for the points from that location).  \n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/1/0/f10d405be0022ba6d169c9c3be25ce80878bd153.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"[asy] label(&quot;X&quot;, (0,3), NW); label(&quot;Y&quot;, (3,0), SE); label(&quot;1&quot;, (2,0), SE); label(&quot;1&quot;, (1,0), SW); label(&quot;1&quot;, (0,0), SW); label(&quot;4&quot;, (0,1), SW); label(&quot;10&quot;, (0,2), NW); label(&quot;3&quot;, (1,1), SW); label(&quot;2&quot;, (2,1), SE); label(&quot;1&quot;, (3,1), SE); label(&quot;1&quot;, (3,2), NE); label(&quot;1&quot;, (3,3), NE); label(&quot;3&quot;, (2,2), NE); label(&quot;4&quot;, (2,3), NE); label(&quot;10&quot;, (1,3), NW); label(&quot;6&quot;, (1,2), NW); Draw((0,0) -- (3,0) -- (3,1) -- (3,3) -- (1,3) -- (0,3) -- cycle, linewidth(1)); Draw((1,0)--(1,3), linewidth(1)); Draw((2,0)--(2,3), linewidth(1)); Draw((3,0)--(3,3), linewidth(1)); Draw((0,1)--(3,1), linewidth(1)); Draw((0,2)--(3,2), linewidth(1)); Draw((0,3)--(3,3), linewidth(1)); [/asy]\" width=\"252\" height=\"245\" />\n</p><p>Now we evaluate <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/c/7/2c7774174993ffa29a38cf577ae34d8e0a3a63cb.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$XY$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"33\" height=\"14\" />.  For <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/c/7/2c7774174993ffa29a38cf577ae34d8e0a3a63cb.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$XY$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"33\" height=\"14\" />, we have two cases.  We can go down, or right.  When we go right, we get O which has 10 possible ways from there.  When we go down, we get G which has 10 possible ways.  Therefore <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/c/7/2c7774174993ffa29a38cf577ae34d8e0a3a63cb.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$XY$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"33\" height=\"14\" /> has <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/8/b/c8bf3c869ee37d06e7ee0ec276b06aa2f02027c2.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\boxed{20}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"30\" height=\"23\" /> possibles ways when only going right or down.  \n</p><p><br />\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/6/5/b65051d693426ba5d4f374a16c1662883ebc6181.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"[asy] label(&quot;20&quot;, (0,3), NW); label(&quot;Y&quot;, (3,0), SE); label(&quot;1&quot;, (2,0), SE); label(&quot;1&quot;, (1,0), SW); label(&quot;1&quot;, (0,0), SW); label(&quot;4&quot;, (0,1), SW); label(&quot;10&quot;, (0,2), NW); label(&quot;3&quot;, (1,1), SW); label(&quot;2&quot;, (2,1), SE); label(&quot;1&quot;, (3,1), SE); label(&quot;1&quot;, (3,2), NE); label(&quot;1&quot;, (3,3), NE); label(&quot;3&quot;, (2,2), NE); label(&quot;4&quot;, (2,3), NE); label(&quot;10&quot;, (1,3), NW); label(&quot;6&quot;, (1,2), NW); Draw((0,0) -- (3,0) -- (3,1) -- (3,3) -- (1,3) -- (0,3) -- cycle, linewidth(1)); Draw((1,0)--(1,3), linewidth(1)); Draw((2,0)--(2,3), linewidth(1)); Draw((3,0)--(3,3), linewidth(1)); Draw((0,1)--(3,1), linewidth(1)); Draw((0,2)--(3,2), linewidth(1)); Draw((0,3)--(3,3), linewidth(1)); [/asy]\" width=\"252\" height=\"245\" />\n</p>\n<h1><span id=\"How_does_block_walking_even_work.3F\"></span><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"How_does_block_walking_even_work?\">How does block walking even work?</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Block_walking&amp;action=edit&amp;section=3\" title=\"Edit section: How does block walking even work?\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h1>\n<p><b>At this point, you may be wondering, how does block walking work? </b> \nWhen you are restricted to certain boundaries of only moving two different directions, the first move from a certain spot is going to have to be one of those two directions.  We start off at the beginning, where we know how to count the cases.  From there, we can begin filling in more boxes, until we get to the final point.  These boxes are filled out, knowing that we have to start by going one specific direction.  Since we already have this information filled out, we can just find the sum of the two different directions.  We continue doing this, until we get to the area that we want to find the amount of ways.  We will already have the two different directions marked by this time, so we can just add these two points to get our final result.\n</p>\n<h1><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"A_Question_to_think_about\">A Question to think about</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Block_walking&amp;action=edit&amp;section=4\" title=\"Edit section: A Question to think about\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h1>\n<ul><li>What pattern do these numbers have?  (Hint: try rotating your head 135 degrees)  Can you explain this? (No my neck broken)</li></ul>\n<!-- \nNewPP limit report\nCached time: 20261018074744\nCache expiry: 86400\nReduced expiry: false\nComplications: [show‐toc]\nCPU time usage: 0.019 seconds\nReal time usage: 0.030 seconds\nPreprocessor visited node count: 191/1000000\nPost‐expand include size: 0/2097152 bytes\nTemplate argument size: 0/2097152 bytes\nHighest expansion depth: 2/100\nExpensive parser function count: 0/100\nUnstrip recursion depth: 0/20\nUnstrip post‐expand size: 13424/5000000 bytes\n-->\n<!--\nTransclusion expansion time report (%,ms,calls,template)\n100.00%    0.000      1 -total\n-->\n\n<!-- Saved in parser cache with key wiki-wiki_:pcache:9498:|#|:idhash:canonical and timestamp 20261018074744 and revision id 241679. Rende